      "text": "To find the area of the figure \\(ABCD\\), we start by understanding that it comprises two identical isosceles triangles connected at their bases.\n\n1. **Determine each sector's area:**\n\n   **Angle:**\n   The central angle of each sector is 60 degrees, which corresponds to a full 360-degree circle. The measurement for the arcs created by these sectors is 60 degrees, thus the radius is:\n\n   $$\n   \\left(\\frac{60}{360}\\right)2\\pi \\cdot 12 = 12\\pi/3 = 4\\pi \\text{ radians.}\n   $$\n\n   **Area of a Sector:**\n\n   The area of a sector of a circle given the central angle and radius in radians is:\n\n   \\[\n   (1/2)(r^2)\\theta\n   \\]\n   where \\(r\\) is the radius and \\(\\theta\\) is the central angle:\n\n   \\[\n   (1/2)(12^2)(4\\pi) = (1/2)(144)(4\\pi) = 288\\pi/2 = 144\\pi\n   \\]\n\n2. **Combine the sectors' areas:**\n\n   Two sectors are placed side by side, sharing a common base. Thus, the area of figure \\(ABCD\\) is:\n\n   \\[\n   2 \\times (144\\pi) = 288\\pi\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the area of \\(ABCD\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{288\\pi}\n\\]",
